    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d729">

                <pb facs="0762" n="750" sameAs="frus:frus1945v08/pg750"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">867N.01/9–2945: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">
                        Amir Abdullah of Trans-Jordan to President
                            Truman
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Amman</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-09-29T14:00:00+02:00">September 29,
                            1945—2 p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>The report in the press of the Middle East that Your Excellency asked the Prime
                    Minister of Great Britain in a personal message to grant 10,000 [<hi
                        rendition="#italic">100,000</hi>] immigration certificates to the Jews has
                    caused great anxiety here. I had the honour of receiving a reply from you to my
                    telegram to the late President<anchor n="47" xml:id="d729-f1"/> to the effect
                    that no decision would be taken about Palestine before the Arabs and Jews had
                    been consulted but the admission of 10,000 [<hi rendition="#italic">10,000</hi>]
                    Jewish immigrants is so important a decision that the Arabs consider that they
                    ought to be consulted. I hope to receive a favourable reply which will remove
                    this anxiety.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Abdullah Ibn Hussein</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d729-f1" n="47" xml:id="d729-f1-content">
                <p>March 10, 1945. not printed, but see footnote 39, <ref target="#pg692">p.
                        692</ref>. For President Truman’s message of May 17, see <ref
